6 opcodes means 3 bits 110, two registers is 1 bit
register a is 0, b is 1
hlf 0000000x, therefore 0 and register bit
tpl 0001000x, therefore 1 ..
inc 0010000x, therefore 2 ..
jmp 01000000, therefore 3, last 6 bits are offset

if top bit set we check for 0 or 1, ignore register
jie 10000000, and the last 6 bits are offset
jio 11000000, and the last 6 bits are offset

offset is 6 bits:
0 00000 / first bit is the sign, and the next is the number, so -32/32

Since jmp doesnt take a register and jie and jio always use register 1, we can 
get away with 6 bits offset, using 1 bit for sign and 2 bits for op code

Otherwise to make offsets work we would need to save a null byte for every
opcode that didnt use offsets to make jumping possible using program counter.
Alt. we could jump by reading offsets and calculate while moving backwards or
forwards, but this would be more logic. 

/*
   hlf r sets register r to half its current value, then continues with the next instruction.
   tpl r sets register r to triple its current value, then continues with the next instruction.
   inc r increments register r, adding 1 to it, then continues with the next instruction.
   jie r, offset is like jmp, but only jumps if register r is even ("jump if even").
   jio r, offset is like jmp, but only jumps if register r is 1 ("jump if one", not odd).
   jmp offset is a jump; it continues with the instruction offset away relative to itself.

   */

